@preamble let profile = import("profile")
counts
Measures Aiki-level computational work performed while function executes.
The function takes no arguments. The result is ordinary Aiki data: a list of
[unit, count] pairs for arithmetic, comparison, call, iteration, index, send,
receive, store_read, and store_write. The call to function itself is included.
Setup performed before counts is outside the measurement.

counts(function)

let work = () { return 1 + 2 }
profile.counts(work)[0]    # [:arithmetic, 1]
===
measure
Measures the same semantic units as counts, but also retains the Aiki source
sites at which work occurred. The result is [counts, sites]. Each site entry is
[unit, count, file, line, column, function, detail, source]. Detail identifies
the called function for call sites when available. This richer form is opt-in;
counts does not pay the source-attribution cost.

measure(function)

let work = () { return 1 + 2 }
profile.measure(work)[0][0]    # [:arithmetic, 1]
===
experiment
Measures function(size) and returns [size, counts]. If size is a list, each
supplied value is measured independently and a list of results is returned.
The profiler assigns no meaning to size; the measured function does.

experiment(size, function)

let work = (n) { return n + 1 }
profile.experiment(10, work)[0]    # 10
===
complexity
@unchecked
Classifies the observed growth of each semantic unit in results returned by
experiment over at least three positive sizes. Candidate families are constant,
logarithmic, linear, n-log-n, quadratic, and cubic. Each returned entry is
[unit, growth, score]. A lower score means count/growth(size) remained more
stable across the supplied sizes. Units with no observed work report :none.
The result is empirical: it reports growth consistent with the observations;
it is not a proof of asymptotic complexity. The original experiment results
remain ordinary Aiki data and should be inspected alongside the classification.

complexity(results)

let work = (n) {
	let i = 0
	while i < n {
		i = i + 1
	}
}
let results = profile.experiment([10, 20, 40, 80], work)
profile.complexity(results)[0][1]    # :linear
